<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Napir-Asu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">14th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century-bce">14th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Napir-Asu was a queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a> in the fourteenth century BCE, wife of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/untash-napirisha">Untash-Napirisha</a>. She is known chiefly from her life-size bronze statue found at Susa in 1903, cast over a solid core and weighing about 1,750 kilograms, now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a>. Although its head is lost, the statue, inscribed with curses against anyone who damaged it, is among the largest metal sculptures surviving from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">ancient Near East</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/napir-asu.jpg" alt="Napir-Asu" width="320" height="438" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Shonagon · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue_de_Napir-Asu_-_Mus%C3%A9e_du_Louvre_-_Antiquit%C3%A9s_orientales_SB_2731_-_photo_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
